title: Writing a schema
tags: Schema
kind: guide
summary: Edit .metafolder/schema.json, give metarecords their types with mf_schema, reload, and check what the data already violates.

A new repository starts with a copy of the shipped default schema,
`.metafolder/schema.json`, so that the GUI's type picker works at once. Edit it, or
write your own ([[Schema file]]):

```
{"version": 1,
 "groups": [
   {"targets": "*",        "constraints": [{"field": "rating", "type": "int"}]},
   {"targets": ["film"],   "constraints": [{"field": "rating", "type": "int", "min": 0, "max": 1},
                                           {"field": "director", "type": "ref"}]},
   {"targets": ["person", "film"],
                           "constraints": [{"field": "name", "type": "string", "min": 1, "max": 1}]}]}
```

That reads: wherever `rating` appears it is an int; a film has at most one; a
person and a film each have exactly one `name`, a string.

Give a metarecord its type with `mf_schema`, a multi-valued field — a metarecord
can have several types, whose constraints add up:

```
mf -n films metarecord -i <uuid> field add mf_schema:string=film
mf -n films metarecord -q 'mf_schema = "film"' get          # every film
```

Then apply the edited file and see what the existing data violates:

```
mf -n films schema reload      # an invalid file is refused, the old schema stays
mf -n films schema check       # every violation (exit code 1 if any)
mf -n films schema check 'mf_schema = "film"'
```

Editing the schema never rewrites or blocks existing data: a violation only
blocks a later write that touches the violating field ([[Delta validation]]).
